WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.840
Push a small bead into the back of a chick embryo's forebrain.

2
00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:10.100
It is soaked in one protein, FGF8.

3
00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.620
A second midbrain can grow there, built back to front.

4
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:15.680
Who tells cells what to build?

5
00:00:19.083 --> 00:00:22.603
Hox genes give the hindbrain and spinal cord their addresses.

6
00:00:23.543 --> 00:00:26.003
The midbrain and forebrain have no Hox code at all.

7
00:00:26.933 --> 00:00:31.313
Front at the left: forebrain, then midbrain, then a narrowing called the isthmus.

8
00:00:32.273 --> 00:00:36.033
Behind it, the roof of rhombomere 1 gives rise to the cerebellum.

9
00:00:36.917 --> 00:00:41.117
A local organizer is a small signaling center inside the tube.

10
00:00:41.917 --> 00:00:43.657
It tells nearby cells what to build.

11
00:00:44.497 --> 00:00:46.057
The best studied sits at the isthmus.

12
00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:48.690
It comes down to one idea.

13
00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:50.630
A line becomes a source.

14
00:00:51.683 --> 00:00:57.083
Where Otx2 meets Gbx2, the boundary cells become a signaling source.

15
00:00:58.023 --> 00:00:59.623
FGF8 is the chief signal.

16
00:01:00.583 --> 00:01:04.983
On the simplest reading, how much signal a cell gets tells it which part to build.

17
00:01:05.843 --> 00:01:08.523
The repressor it carries sets which region it can become.

18
00:01:09.383 --> 00:01:13.343
In the roof of the tube, that means midbrain in front and cerebellum behind.

19
00:01:17.817 --> 00:01:18.877
Start with the line.

20
00:01:20.157 --> 00:01:22.557
Otx2 is on in the forebrain and midbrain.

21
00:01:23.957 --> 00:01:26.097
Gbx2 is on in the front of the hindbrain.

22
00:01:27.037 --> 00:01:28.157
Each represses the other.

23
00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:33.100
A cell with a little more Otx2 makes less Gbx2.

24
00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:35.180
Its neighbour does the opposite.

25
00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:39.300
So a blurred edge can sharpen, and the line sits where the two meet.

26
00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.240
How sharp depends on how strong and switch-like the repression is.

27
00:01:44.183 --> 00:01:49.363
Predict. In a mouse, force Otx2 on in the front of the hindbrain.

28
00:01:50.303 --> 00:01:51.303
Where should the line go?

29
00:01:51.983 --> 00:01:53.443
What happens to the cerebellum?

30
00:01:59.767 --> 00:02:02.967
The line moves back, with its band of FGF8.

31
00:02:03.807 --> 00:02:05.107
The cerebellum is smaller.

32
00:02:06.147 --> 00:02:09.247
Broccoli, Boncinelli and Wurst showed this in 1999.

33
00:02:10.467 --> 00:02:12.447
Millet and colleagues did the reverse.

34
00:02:13.887 --> 00:02:17.187
Gbx2 made in the back of the midbrain moved the line forward.

35
00:02:20.667 --> 00:02:22.027
This is a move-it test.

36
00:02:22.967 --> 00:02:25.627
Change where the players are, and see if the pattern follows.

37
00:02:26.587 --> 00:02:28.147
The line follows the repressors.

38
00:02:29.067 --> 00:02:35.127
Right at the line, cells make secreted signals: FGF8 just behind it, Wnt1 just in

39
00:02:35.127 --> 00:02:37.667
front. This line is more than a fence.

40
00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:41.040
Takeaway: two repressors place the line.

41
00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:43.080
Next, is that line a source?
